\DOC LOCAL_MONTSQR_P384_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTSQR_P384_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style symbolic execution goal across an inlined P-384
Montgomery squaring block, replacing the current-state assumptions with ones
about a later state and adding a conditional specification of the squaring
result.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects a variant of the block being stepped
over; both values occur in the observed traces, with no visible difference in
the shape of the outcome. The second argument, a {string list}, names the
result variable and the input variable of the squaring step; these names appear
in the newly introduced specification assumption and in the memory-read
assumption recording where the result is stored.
{
   A ?- eventually ... s
   ================================  LOCAL_MONTSQR_P384_TAC n [res; inp]
   A' ?- eventually ... s'
}
\noindent Here {s'} is a fresh later state variable and the {eventually} body
is otherwise unchanged. In {A'}, every assumption of {A} mentioning the old
state — the program-counter read, {bytes_loaded}, register and memory reads,
and the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} frame relating the initial state to the current
one — is discharged and re-added with the new state variable, the program
counter advanced and the {MAYCHANGE} frame extended by the component written
to; the remaining mathematical assumptions are retained, possibly reordered.
{A'} also gains an implication whose hypothesis bounds the square of the input
and whose conclusion equates the named result variable with the
Montgomery-reduced square modulo {p_384}, together with a memory-read
assumption giving that result at the destination address.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour,
always on goals of this {eventually} form carrying a full set of machine-state
assumptions.

\ENDDOC
